-- title:
--   Cones, double cones, configurations, and admissibility
-- statement:
--   Let $E$ be a real inner product space. For $v \in E$ and an angle $\vartheta$, the **cone** and the **double cone** are
--
--   $$\tilde V(v,\vartheta) = \Big\{h \neq 0 \ :\ \frac{\langle v, h\rangle}{\|h\|} > \cos\vartheta\Big\}, \qquad V(v,\vartheta) = \tilde V(v,\vartheta) \cup \big(-\tilde V(v,\vartheta)\big).$$
--
--   Neither contains the origin, and both are open. When $\|v\| = 1$ and $0 \le \vartheta \le \pi$ these admit the angular description: $h \in \tilde V(v,\vartheta)$ iff $h \ne 0$ and the unoriented angle $\angle(v,h)$ is less than $\vartheta$, and $h \in V(v,\vartheta)$ iff $h \ne 0$ and either $\angle(v,h) < \vartheta$ or $\angle(v,h) > \pi - \vartheta$. The angular form is what carries a triangle inequality, which the covering arguments need.
--
--   A **double cone as an object** is a bundle of a unit axis $v$ and an apex angle $\vartheta$ with $0 < \vartheta \le \pi/2$; its **carrier** is the set $V(v,\vartheta)$, and membership in the object means membership in the carrier. In particular $\vartheta \le \pi$ and the origin is never a member.
--
--   A **configuration** is a map $\Gamma : E \to \{\text{double cones}\}$. It is **$\vartheta$-bounded** when $\vartheta > 0$ and $\vartheta \le \Gamma(x).\mathrm{apex}$ for every $x$, so $\vartheta$ is a uniform positive lower bound on the apex angles occurring. The cone based at a point is $V^\Gamma[x] = x + \Gamma(x)$, so that $y \in V^\Gamma[x]$ iff $y - x$ lies in the carrier of $\Gamma(x)$; since the origin is never in a carrier, $x \notin V^\Gamma[x]$ — a configuration puts no loop at a point. When $E$ carries a measurable structure, $\Gamma$ satisfies **condition (M)** when
--
--   $$\{(x,y) \in E \times E \ :\ y - x \in \Gamma(x)\}$$
--
--   is a measurable set, and $\Gamma$ is **$\vartheta$-admissible** when it is $\vartheta$-bounded and satisfies (M).
--
--   These are the base objects on which everything else in the development rests: a configuration is the geometric datum that a jump kernel is required to see from below, boundedness is the uniform-aperture hypothesis that makes the constants depend on $\vartheta$ alone, and condition (M) is the measurability hypothesis that lets the cone data enter an integral.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The double cone is represented as a structure carrying a *unit axis* rather than a point of projective space, so two distinct structures can name the same subset of $E$ ($v$ and $-v$ give the same double cone). Wherever cone *types* are compared, the comparison is therefore made between carriers rather than between structures. The bare cone definitions place no constraint on $v$ or $\vartheta$; the constraints live in the bundled structure.
